1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,896
A G P U listed at three dollars seventeen an hour can

2
00:00:03,896 --> 00:00:06,399
really cost sixty-three dollars forty.

3
00:00:06,399 --> 00:00:07,411
Same machine,

4
00:00:07,411 --> 00:00:08,424
same invoice.

5
00:00:08,424 --> 00:00:11,880
The only thing that changed is how much of the time it

6
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:13,430
was doing useful work.

7
00:00:13,430 --> 00:00:16,290
And one line in a prompt can triple an A P I

8
00:00:16,290 --> 00:00:18,792
bill without changing a single answer.

9
00:00:19,142 --> 00:00:20,674
This is the cost lesson.

10
00:00:20,674 --> 00:00:24,173
Every number comes from arithmetic on the notebook's prices,

11
00:00:24,173 --> 00:00:25,869
or from a small test I ran.

12
00:00:25,869 --> 00:00:29,478
You will leave knowing where the money goes and which lever to

13
00:00:29,478 --> 00:00:30,298
pull first.

14
00:00:31,548 --> 00:00:35,156
Welcome to lesson eleven of the cloud for A I engineers course,

15
00:00:35,156 --> 00:00:36,503
on The Code Notebook.

16
00:00:36,853 --> 00:00:39,987
The handwritten notes and the code for this lesson are linked in

17
00:00:39,987 --> 00:00:40,909
the description.

18
00:00:42,159 --> 00:00:42,881
We built,

19
00:00:42,881 --> 00:00:43,991
trained, served,

20
00:00:43,991 --> 00:00:46,101
shipped and secured the assistant.

21
00:00:46,101 --> 00:00:47,989
Now someone has to pay for it.

22
00:00:47,989 --> 00:00:51,209
The surprise in A I costs is that the biggest waste is

23
00:00:51,209 --> 00:00:52,874
rarely the price per hour.

24
00:00:52,874 --> 00:00:54,984
It is hours that nobody was using,

25
00:00:54,984 --> 00:00:57,538
and tokens that were sent again and again.

26
00:00:57,888 --> 00:00:58,990
Honest labels.

27
00:00:58,990 --> 00:01:03,890
Prices are the notebook's approximate list prices from September and October

28
00:01:03,890 --> 00:01:05,237
twenty twenty-six,

29
00:01:05,237 --> 00:01:06,707
and they move often.

30
00:01:06,707 --> 00:01:08,421
The sums are arithmetic.

31
00:01:08,421 --> 00:01:11,116
The cache test uses a toy cache I wrote.

32
00:01:11,116 --> 00:01:13,443
The alert test uses invented logs.

33
00:01:14,693 --> 00:01:16,603
Fin Ops means engineering,

34
00:01:16,603 --> 00:01:20,233
finance and product sharing ownership of cloud spend,

35
00:01:20,233 --> 00:01:22,462
so cost becomes a design input,

36
00:01:22,462 --> 00:01:23,544
like latency.

37
00:01:23,544 --> 00:01:24,945
It runs as a loop.

38
00:01:24,945 --> 00:01:26,282
Inform, optimise,

39
00:01:26,282 --> 00:01:28,829
operate. Inform is seeing the spend,

40
00:01:28,829 --> 00:01:32,650
tagging G P Us and metering tokens per team and feature.

41
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:35,851
Optimise fixes the rate and the amount used.

42
00:01:35,851 --> 00:01:38,881
Operate keeps it fixed with budgets and alerts.

43
00:01:38,881 --> 00:01:40,248
A memory hook is I,

44
00:01:40,248 --> 00:01:40,782
O, O.

45
00:01:40,782 --> 00:01:41,436
See it,

46
00:01:41,436 --> 00:01:42,089
fix it,

47
00:01:42,089 --> 00:01:43,158
keep it fixed.

48
00:01:43,158 --> 00:01:46,248
You cannot optimise what you have not allocated,

49
00:01:46,248 --> 00:01:48,802
and savings do not last without alerts.

50
00:01:50,052 --> 00:01:52,404
First, measure utilisation honestly.

51
00:01:52,404 --> 00:01:55,932
The number you see in nvidia dash smi says what share of

52
00:01:55,932 --> 00:01:58,990
the last second had at least one kernel running.

53
00:01:58,990 --> 00:02:02,753
A tiny kernel on a single processing block reads the same as

54
00:02:02,753 --> 00:02:04,575
one filling the whole chip.

55
00:02:04,575 --> 00:02:07,751
So a G P U can show one hundred percent while most

56
00:02:07,751 --> 00:02:08,927
of it sits idle.

57
00:02:09,277 --> 00:02:12,710
Better is the D C G M profiling field called S M

58
00:02:12,710 --> 00:02:16,144
active, the fraction of time warps were running,

59
00:02:16,144 --> 00:02:19,049
averaged over all the processing blocks.

60
00:02:19,049 --> 00:02:20,436
Watch S M active,

61
00:02:20,436 --> 00:02:23,077
occupancy, and tensor pipe activity.

62
00:02:23,077 --> 00:02:26,313
Above eighty percent S M active is necessary,

63
00:02:26,313 --> 00:02:27,567
not sufficient.

64
00:02:28,817 --> 00:02:30,209
How idle is normal?

65
00:02:30,209 --> 00:02:34,688
A vendor study of tens of thousands of Kubernetes clusters that nobody

66
00:02:34,688 --> 00:02:38,440
had optimised found average G P U use around five percent.

67
00:02:38,440 --> 00:02:40,195
That is vendor telemetry,

68
00:02:40,195 --> 00:02:41,950
so treat it as a warning,

69
00:02:41,950 --> 00:02:42,798
not a law.

70
00:02:42,798 --> 00:02:47,518
A survey of five hundred seventy-three leaders found eighty-six percent of

71
00:02:47,518 --> 00:02:51,573
enterprises with their own G P Us report fifty percent or less.

72
00:02:51,923 --> 00:02:56,027
Another survey found forty-four percent assign G P Us by hand or

73
00:02:56,027 --> 00:02:57,295
have no strategy.

74
00:02:57,295 --> 00:03:00,252
So if your cluster looks busy on a dashboard,

75
00:03:00,252 --> 00:03:02,848
check which number the dashboard shows.

76
00:03:02,848 --> 00:03:04,477
Then fix idle capacity,

77
00:03:04,477 --> 00:03:06,771
because that is the biggest lever.

78
00:03:08,021 --> 00:03:10,785
The real price of a G P U hour is the list

79
00:03:10,785 --> 00:03:12,769
price divided by utilisation.

80
00:03:12,769 --> 00:03:15,534
At the three dollar seventeen index price,

81
00:03:15,534 --> 00:03:19,861
five percent use makes a useful hour cost sixty-three dollars forty.

82
00:03:19,861 --> 00:03:21,243
Twenty-two percent,

83
00:03:21,243 --> 00:03:22,626
fourteen forty-one.

84
00:03:22,626 --> 00:03:23,708
Fifty percent,

85
00:03:23,708 --> 00:03:24,910
six thirty-four.

86
00:03:24,910 --> 00:03:26,052
Eighty percent,

87
00:03:26,052 --> 00:03:27,314
three ninety-six.

88
00:03:27,664 --> 00:03:31,821
Going from five to fifty percent makes a useful hour ten times

89
00:03:31,821 --> 00:03:36,294
cheaper. The best rate discounts top out around two or three times.

90
00:03:36,294 --> 00:03:39,318
So fix idle time before you negotiate price.

91
00:03:39,318 --> 00:03:43,412
A three dollar H one hundred at five percent is a sixty-three

92
00:03:43,412 --> 00:03:44,987
dollar H one hundred.

93
00:03:46,237 --> 00:03:47,414
Right size next.

94
00:03:47,414 --> 00:03:51,355
If weights and the memory cache fit in ten to twenty gigabytes,

95
00:03:51,355 --> 00:03:54,002
a full eighty gigabyte card is oversized.

96
00:03:54,002 --> 00:03:57,826
A seven billion parameter model at eight bit or four bit with

97
00:03:57,826 --> 00:04:00,061
modest traffic fits a small slice,

98
00:04:00,061 --> 00:04:02,944
or a cheaper card like an L four or L forty S

99
00:04:02,944 --> 00:04:03,826
class part.

100
00:04:04,176 --> 00:04:06,326
There are three ways to share.

101
00:04:06,326 --> 00:04:10,246
M I G cuts hardware slices with their own memory and fault

102
00:04:10,246 --> 00:04:12,523
isolation, up to seven per card,

103
00:04:12,523 --> 00:04:15,368
good for production multi tenant serving.

104
00:04:15,368 --> 00:04:18,909
Time slicing lets pods take turns with no isolation,

105
00:04:18,909 --> 00:04:20,996
fine for trusted development.

106
00:04:20,996 --> 00:04:23,019
M P S runs kernels together,

107
00:04:23,019 --> 00:04:24,663
for trusted workloads.

108
00:04:25,913 --> 00:04:27,534
How you buy matters too.

109
00:04:27,534 --> 00:04:30,543
On demand has no commitment and is the baseline,

110
00:04:30,543 --> 00:04:32,394
good for bursty experiments.

111
00:04:32,394 --> 00:04:36,213
Neoclouds offer a lower list price and the newest chips first.

112
00:04:36,213 --> 00:04:39,627
Reserved commitments give a lower rate for steady load.

113
00:04:39,627 --> 00:04:43,041
Capacity blocks guarantee a cluster for a fixed window,

114
00:04:43,041 --> 00:04:44,661
which is not a discount.

115
00:04:45,011 --> 00:04:48,905
Spot or preemptible capacity is half to seventy percent off,

116
00:04:48,905 --> 00:04:52,311
with notice before eviction of two minutes on A W S,

117
00:04:52,311 --> 00:04:54,927
and thirty seconds on Azure and Google.

118
00:04:54,927 --> 00:04:58,091
Flex start queues your job until capacity frees.

119
00:04:58,091 --> 00:04:59,855
Every option has a catch.

120
00:04:59,855 --> 00:05:01,498
Pick by workload shape.

121
00:05:02,748 --> 00:05:04,738
The commitment rule is simple.

122
00:05:04,738 --> 00:05:08,308
A commitment at discount d wins only if you use more than

123
00:05:08,308 --> 00:05:10,766
one minus d of the committed capacity.

124
00:05:10,766 --> 00:05:14,804
At a forty percent discount you need more than sixty percent use.

125
00:05:14,804 --> 00:05:17,262
At fifty-five percent for three years,

126
00:05:17,262 --> 00:05:19,194
more than forty-five percent.

127
00:05:19,544 --> 00:05:21,379
I checked it with arithmetic.

128
00:05:21,379 --> 00:05:23,159
At a forty percent discount,

129
00:05:23,159 --> 00:05:25,829
fifty percent use makes the commitment lose,

130
00:05:25,829 --> 00:05:27,886
and seventy percent makes it win.

131
00:05:27,886 --> 00:05:30,667
A discount of d buys you the right to waste d.

132
00:05:30,667 --> 00:05:32,058
With volatile prices,

133
00:05:32,058 --> 00:05:35,673
shorter terms limit the risk of being locked in above market.

134
00:05:36,923 --> 00:05:38,689
Here is one training run.

135
00:05:38,689 --> 00:05:43,745
Sixty-four H one hundreds for fourteen days is twenty-one thousand five hundred

136
00:05:43,745 --> 00:05:45,267
and four G P U hours.

137
00:05:45,267 --> 00:05:50,140
At the index price of three seventeen it costs sixty-eight thousand dollars.

138
00:05:50,140 --> 00:05:51,724
At the market average,

139
00:05:51,724 --> 00:05:53,307
eighty-three thousand.

140
00:05:53,657 --> 00:05:55,537
As an A W S capacity block,

141
00:05:55,537 --> 00:05:58,205
about one hundred twenty-eight thousand.

142
00:05:58,205 --> 00:05:59,963
At hyperscaler on demand,

143
00:05:59,963 --> 00:06:02,570
about one hundred forty-eight thousand.

144
00:06:02,570 --> 00:06:07,178
Spot at sixty percent off is twenty-seven thousand before lost steps and

145
00:06:07,178 --> 00:06:08,572
restarts. Same job,

146
00:06:08,572 --> 00:06:10,876
a spread of five point four times.

147
00:06:10,876 --> 00:06:13,180
Buying is an engineering decision.

148
00:06:14,430 --> 00:06:15,358
Now tokens.

149
00:06:15,358 --> 00:06:18,080
An A P I bill is a sum over token types.

150
00:06:18,080 --> 00:06:20,431
Uncached input at the input price.

151
00:06:20,431 --> 00:06:22,287
Cache writes at a premium.

152
00:06:22,287 --> 00:06:24,143
Cache reads at a fraction.

153
00:06:24,143 --> 00:06:25,504
And output tokens,

154
00:06:25,504 --> 00:06:26,927
including thinking,

155
00:06:26,927 --> 00:06:28,412
at the output price.

156
00:06:28,412 --> 00:06:29,959
Batch jobs cost half.

157
00:06:29,959 --> 00:06:32,557
Some regional options add ten percent.

158
00:06:32,907 --> 00:06:35,430
The shape is the same across vendors.

159
00:06:35,430 --> 00:06:38,323
Output costs about five to six times input.

160
00:06:38,323 --> 00:06:41,154
Cache reads cost a tenth of input or less.

161
00:06:41,154 --> 00:06:42,262
Batch is half.

162
00:06:42,262 --> 00:06:45,709
So the question is never just the price per million.

163
00:06:45,709 --> 00:06:49,217
It is how many tokens of each kind each request uses.

164
00:06:50,467 --> 00:06:54,179
Here are list prices per million tokens from the notebook,

165
00:06:54,179 --> 00:06:55,556
October fourteenth,

166
00:06:55,556 --> 00:06:56,873
twenty twenty-six.

167
00:06:56,873 --> 00:07:00,585
Opus five point five costs four dollars in and twenty out.

168
00:07:00,585 --> 00:07:02,201
Sonnet five point five,

169
00:07:02,201 --> 00:07:03,578
two in and ten out.

170
00:07:03,578 --> 00:07:05,135
Haiku five point five,

171
00:07:05,135 --> 00:07:07,350
ten cents in and fifty cents out.

172
00:07:07,700 --> 00:07:11,164
Cache reads are five percent of input on Opus and Sonnet,

173
00:07:11,164 --> 00:07:12,812
and ten percent on Haiku.

174
00:07:12,812 --> 00:07:16,674
Writing a five minute cache costs one and a quarter times input.

175
00:07:16,674 --> 00:07:18,094
These figures change,

176
00:07:18,094 --> 00:07:20,934
so treat the table as an example of the shape,

177
00:07:20,934 --> 00:07:23,604
and check the vendor page before budgeting.

178
00:07:24,854 --> 00:07:26,714
Let me cost the support bot.

179
00:07:26,714 --> 00:07:30,726
Each request has a three thousand token system prompt that can be

180
00:07:30,726 --> 00:07:34,389
cached, a thousand uncached tokens of history and question,

181
00:07:34,389 --> 00:07:36,598
and four hundred tokens of output.

182
00:07:36,598 --> 00:07:39,157
On Sonnet five point five with no cache,

183
00:07:39,157 --> 00:07:41,017
that is one point two cents,

184
00:07:41,017 --> 00:07:43,866
twelve thousand dollars per million requests.

185
00:07:44,216 --> 00:07:45,492
When the cache hits,

186
00:07:45,492 --> 00:07:47,777
it drops to six thousand three hundred.

187
00:07:47,777 --> 00:07:48,522
A cascade,

188
00:07:48,522 --> 00:07:51,817
where Haiku answers first and thirty percent go to Sonnet,

189
00:07:51,817 --> 00:07:53,571
is four thousand two hundred.

190
00:07:53,571 --> 00:07:56,813
Batch on top of a cache hit is three thousand one hundred

191
00:07:56,813 --> 00:07:59,259
fifty. The model tier moves the bill most,

192
00:07:59,259 --> 00:08:00,162
then caching,

193
00:08:00,162 --> 00:08:00,959
then batch.

194
00:08:02,209 --> 00:08:03,661
Caching is lever one.

195
00:08:03,661 --> 00:08:07,320
A five minute cache write costs one and a quarter times the

196
00:08:07,320 --> 00:08:08,249
input price,

197
00:08:08,249 --> 00:08:10,688
and a read costs a tenth or twentieth.

198
00:08:10,688 --> 00:08:13,998
So a five minute cache pays back after a single read,

199
00:08:13,998 --> 00:08:16,030
and a one hour cache after two.

200
00:08:16,030 --> 00:08:20,734
Vendors report agent loops reading a median eighty-four percent of input from

201
00:08:20,734 --> 00:08:21,315
cache.

202
00:08:21,665 --> 00:08:25,955
In my arithmetic the cache hit saved forty-seven point five percent per

203
00:08:25,955 --> 00:08:29,331
request. And at a hit rate of fifty percent the bill is

204
00:08:29,331 --> 00:08:31,791
nine thousand nine hundred per million,

205
00:08:31,791 --> 00:08:35,052
at eighty percent seven thousand seven hundred forty,

206
00:08:35,052 --> 00:08:38,370
at ninety-five percent six thousand six hundred sixty.

207
00:08:39,620 --> 00:08:42,516
A cache only helps if requests keep arriving.

208
00:08:42,516 --> 00:08:45,353
If requests share a prefix at a steady rate,

209
00:08:45,353 --> 00:08:48,603
the hit rate is one minus e to the minus rate times

210
00:08:48,603 --> 00:08:51,617
lifetime. I simulated random arrivals to check.

211
00:08:51,617 --> 00:08:55,990
One request a minute with a five minute lifetime hit ninety-nine point

212
00:08:55,990 --> 00:08:57,645
two percent of the time.

213
00:08:57,995 --> 00:09:01,831
One request every ten minutes hit thirty-eight point nine percent,

214
00:09:01,831 --> 00:09:03,968
and one an hour only eight percent.

215
00:09:03,968 --> 00:09:06,050
The formula agreed within a point.

216
00:09:06,050 --> 00:09:09,393
So a quiet feature can pay for cache writes and never get

217
00:09:09,393 --> 00:09:10,160
the reads.

218
00:09:10,160 --> 00:09:11,804
Measure the real hit rate,

219
00:09:11,804 --> 00:09:13,393
then decide the lifetime.

220
00:09:14,643 --> 00:09:17,133
Here is the mistake that triples bills.

221
00:09:17,133 --> 00:09:19,564
A cache matches the start of a prompt.

222
00:09:19,564 --> 00:09:22,748
If a timestamp or user name sits at the very top of

223
00:09:22,748 --> 00:09:24,021
the system prompt,

224
00:09:24,021 --> 00:09:26,163
every request starts differently,

225
00:09:26,163 --> 00:09:27,958
and nothing is ever reused.

226
00:09:27,958 --> 00:09:31,720
I wrote a toy prefix cache and fed it five thousand requests.

227
00:09:32,070 --> 00:09:33,838
With the timestamp on top,

228
00:09:33,838 --> 00:09:36,784
zero percent of tokens were served from cache.

229
00:09:36,784 --> 00:09:39,730
With the same timestamp after the shared part,

230
00:09:39,730 --> 00:09:41,497
seventy-five percent were.

231
00:09:41,497 --> 00:09:44,620
The input bill was three and a half times higher.

232
00:09:44,620 --> 00:09:46,270
Rule. Stable text first,

233
00:09:46,270 --> 00:09:47,625
changing text last.

234
00:09:48,875 --> 00:09:50,248
Lever two is batch.

235
00:09:50,248 --> 00:09:53,650
Batch processing takes fifty percent off every token,

236
00:09:53,650 --> 00:09:55,202
including cached ones,

237
00:09:55,202 --> 00:09:58,307
in exchange for waiting up to twenty-four hours.

238
00:09:58,307 --> 00:10:01,530
It is the second biggest free lever after caching.

239
00:10:01,880 --> 00:10:05,572
Anything that does not need an instant answer belongs there.

240
00:10:05,572 --> 00:10:07,188
Offline evaluation runs,

241
00:10:07,188 --> 00:10:09,091
backfills, nightly labelling,

242
00:10:09,091 --> 00:10:11,168
summarising yesterday's tickets.

243
00:10:11,168 --> 00:10:12,495
On the support bot,

244
00:10:12,495 --> 00:10:16,014
batch on top of a cache hit halves the three thousand per

245
00:10:16,014 --> 00:10:17,053
million again.

246
00:10:18,303 --> 00:10:21,629
Levers three and four are the prompt audit and effort.

247
00:10:21,629 --> 00:10:22,948
Trim what you send.

248
00:10:22,948 --> 00:10:27,020
Searching a tool list instead of sending all five hundred tools cut

249
00:10:27,020 --> 00:10:29,830
input forty-five percent in a published test.

250
00:10:29,830 --> 00:10:32,640
Pruning stale tool results saved thirty-nine,

251
00:10:32,640 --> 00:10:34,819
and a plain prompt audit fourteen.

252
00:10:35,169 --> 00:10:36,711
Effort is a price dial.

253
00:10:36,711 --> 00:10:37,911
On one benchmark,

254
00:10:37,911 --> 00:10:41,623
a cheaper model at high effort solved nearly as many tasks as

255
00:10:41,623 --> 00:10:43,393
a bigger one at low effort,

256
00:10:43,393 --> 00:10:44,936
at about the same cost.

257
00:10:44,936 --> 00:10:49,505
Running everything at low effort and re-running failures at high saved about

258
00:10:49,505 --> 00:10:50,533
forty percent,

259
00:10:50,533 --> 00:10:52,874
but only if you can detect a failure,

260
00:10:52,874 --> 00:10:54,245
like a failing test.

261
00:10:55,495 --> 00:10:57,129
Lever five comes last.

262
00:10:57,129 --> 00:11:01,215
Routing sends easy requests to a cheap model and hard ones to

263
00:11:01,215 --> 00:11:02,221
a big model.

264
00:11:02,221 --> 00:11:03,855
In the notebook's sum,

265
00:11:03,855 --> 00:11:08,821
Haiku first with thirty percent escalated to Sonnet cuts sixty-five percent

266
00:11:08,821 --> 00:11:10,267
against all Sonnet.

267
00:11:10,267 --> 00:11:14,101
That holds only if the escalation classifier is reliable.

268
00:11:14,451 --> 00:11:17,996
Quality on the easy seventy percent must stay acceptable.

269
00:11:17,996 --> 00:11:19,448
Caches are per model.

270
00:11:19,448 --> 00:11:21,598
And escalated requests pay twice.

271
00:11:21,598 --> 00:11:25,897
So compare against the best single model at lower effort with caching.

272
00:11:25,897 --> 00:11:28,744
Routing is the most common lever in industry,

273
00:11:28,744 --> 00:11:31,998
and the one most often done before the cheaper ones.

274
00:11:33,248 --> 00:11:35,545
Should you host the model yourself?

275
00:11:35,545 --> 00:11:37,136
Start with the formula.

276
00:11:37,136 --> 00:11:40,848
Dollars per million tokens equals the G P U price per hour,

277
00:11:40,848 --> 00:11:45,796
divided by tokens per second times three thousand six hundred times utilisation,

278
00:11:45,796 --> 00:11:46,975
times a million.

279
00:11:46,975 --> 00:11:50,568
A seventy billion model at eight bit on one H one hundred

280
00:11:50,568 --> 00:11:53,160
does about four hundred tokens a second,

281
00:11:53,160 --> 00:11:55,517
a vendor figure likely conservative.

282
00:11:55,867 --> 00:11:59,599
At full use that is two dollars twenty per million tokens.

283
00:11:59,599 --> 00:12:00,863
At sixty percent,

284
00:12:00,863 --> 00:12:02,187
three sixty-seven.

285
00:12:02,187 --> 00:12:03,451
At forty percent,

286
00:12:03,451 --> 00:12:04,354
five fifty.

287
00:12:04,354 --> 00:12:05,739
At fifteen percent,

288
00:12:05,739 --> 00:12:07,243
fourteen sixty-eight.

289
00:12:07,243 --> 00:12:09,711
Utilisation again decides everything,

290
00:12:09,711 --> 00:12:13,082
and production use is often thirty to sixty percent.

291
00:12:14,332 --> 00:12:16,120
Now the break-even volume.

292
00:12:16,120 --> 00:12:20,650
One H one hundred running all day costs seventy-six dollars eight cents.

293
00:12:20,650 --> 00:12:22,915
Divide by the A P I blended price.

294
00:12:22,915 --> 00:12:23,928
Against Opus,

295
00:12:23,928 --> 00:12:27,862
self hosting can win above ten point six million tokens a day.

296
00:12:27,862 --> 00:12:28,994
Against Sonnet,

297
00:12:28,994 --> 00:12:31,319
above twenty-one point one million,

298
00:12:31,319 --> 00:12:32,809
with a narrow window,

299
00:12:32,809 --> 00:12:36,564
because one card tops out at thirty-four point six million.

300
00:12:36,914 --> 00:12:38,082
Against Haiku,

301
00:12:38,082 --> 00:12:42,558
the break even is four hundred twenty-three million tokens a day,

302
00:12:42,558 --> 00:12:45,088
far beyond what one card can serve.

303
00:12:45,088 --> 00:12:48,592
So one H one hundred never beats a Haiku class A P

304
00:12:48,592 --> 00:12:50,279
I on G P U cost alone.

305
00:12:50,279 --> 00:12:53,587
Self hosting only competes with premium models.

306
00:12:54,837 --> 00:12:57,535
I also simulated a year of bursty traffic,

307
00:12:57,535 --> 00:12:59,999
averaging twenty million tokens a day,

308
00:12:59,999 --> 00:13:03,166
with one fixed G P U serving the base load and the

309
00:13:03,166 --> 00:13:04,691
A P I taking overflow.

310
00:13:04,691 --> 00:13:09,032
The hybrid cost thirty-one thousand six hundred seventy-eight dollars,

311
00:13:09,032 --> 00:13:12,785
against thirty-one thousand nine hundred nine for all A P I.

312
00:13:12,785 --> 00:13:13,958
Basically a tie,

313
00:13:13,958 --> 00:13:15,249
before any people.

314
00:13:15,599 --> 00:13:17,597
And the sum leaves things out.

315
00:13:17,597 --> 00:13:18,597
On call time,

316
00:13:18,597 --> 00:13:20,302
upgrades, spare replicas,

317
00:13:20,302 --> 00:13:22,888
and the weaker quality of an open model,

318
00:13:22,888 --> 00:13:26,121
which makes cost per successful task the fair test.

319
00:13:26,121 --> 00:13:28,531
The simple sum flatters self hosting.

320
00:13:28,531 --> 00:13:31,706
Price a hosted open model and a batch cached A P I

321
00:13:31,706 --> 00:13:32,294
first.

322
00:13:33,544 --> 00:13:34,773
Now attribution.

323
00:13:34,773 --> 00:13:36,863
G P U cost can use cloud tags,

324
00:13:36,863 --> 00:13:38,215
Kubernetes labels,

325
00:13:38,215 --> 00:13:40,120
and a cost allocation tool.

326
00:13:40,120 --> 00:13:42,148
Tokens have no resource tags,

327
00:13:42,148 --> 00:13:43,931
so a gateway stamps team,

328
00:13:43,931 --> 00:13:46,389
feature and tenant on every request.

329
00:13:46,389 --> 00:13:49,278
Give each project its own key or workspace.

330
00:13:49,278 --> 00:13:52,167
And capture usage when the request happens,

331
00:13:52,167 --> 00:13:54,502
before billing aggregates it away.

332
00:13:54,852 --> 00:13:55,994
Agents fan out,

333
00:13:55,994 --> 00:13:58,519
so join calls with a trace identifier.

334
00:13:58,519 --> 00:14:00,022
Log the token counts,

335
00:14:00,022 --> 00:14:03,989
and compute the dollar cost at log time from a versioned price

336
00:14:03,989 --> 00:14:07,536
table. Never recompute old history with today's prices,

337
00:14:07,536 --> 00:14:11,383
and reconcile against the provider's cost report each month.

338
00:14:12,633 --> 00:14:13,881
With stamped logs,

339
00:14:13,881 --> 00:14:14,958
you can report.

340
00:14:14,958 --> 00:14:18,530
Showback shows each team its cost with no payment enforced,

341
00:14:18,530 --> 00:14:20,231
and is the place to start.

342
00:14:20,231 --> 00:14:23,123
Chargeback lands the cost on the team's budget,

343
00:14:23,123 --> 00:14:25,221
once allocation rules are stable.

344
00:14:25,221 --> 00:14:26,582
In my invented logs,

345
00:14:26,582 --> 00:14:29,814
the triage bot cost about twenty-seven dollars a day,

346
00:14:29,814 --> 00:14:31,232
the order lookup six,

347
00:14:31,232 --> 00:14:33,954
and the refund agent a hundred and fourteen.

348
00:14:34,304 --> 00:14:37,520
The number that matters is cost per resolved ticket.

349
00:14:37,520 --> 00:14:40,622
Cost per conversation divided by the resolve rate.

350
00:14:40,622 --> 00:14:43,609
For the triage bot that came to about six cents,

351
00:14:43,609 --> 00:14:45,332
the lookup under one cent,

352
00:14:45,332 --> 00:14:47,457
the refund agent seventeen cents.

353
00:14:47,457 --> 00:14:49,697
Put it next to the cost of a human,

354
00:14:49,697 --> 00:14:51,018
and you show value,

355
00:14:51,018 --> 00:14:52,109
not just spend.

356
00:14:53,359 --> 00:14:55,549
Operate means budgets and alarms.

357
00:14:55,549 --> 00:14:57,858
Set a monthly budget per workspace,

358
00:14:57,858 --> 00:14:58,982
alert at fifty,

359
00:14:58,982 --> 00:15:01,054
eighty and one hundred percent,

360
00:15:01,054 --> 00:15:04,606
and put hard spend caps at the gateway to stop a runaway

361
00:15:04,606 --> 00:15:06,737
agent. Then watch for anomalies.

362
00:15:06,737 --> 00:15:11,413
A daily cost more than three standard deviations above the trailing average

363
00:15:11,413 --> 00:15:12,893
is a good first rule.

364
00:15:13,243 --> 00:15:16,795
In my invented logs I broke the cache of one feature on

365
00:15:16,795 --> 00:15:19,926
day forty-five by moving a timestamp to the top.

366
00:15:19,926 --> 00:15:22,455
Daily cost rose one point eight times,

367
00:15:22,455 --> 00:15:26,249
and the cache read share fell from seventy percent to zero.

368
00:15:26,249 --> 00:15:28,416
Both alerts fired that same day,

369
00:15:28,416 --> 00:15:31,608
with no false alarms in the two healthy features.

370
00:15:32,858 --> 00:15:35,446
The hidden lines are storage and network.

371
00:15:35,446 --> 00:15:36,194
On A W S,

372
00:15:36,194 --> 00:15:40,566
moving data between availability zones costs about two cents a gigabyte,

373
00:15:40,566 --> 00:15:42,580
counting both send and receive.

374
00:15:42,580 --> 00:15:46,894
A full training checkpoint of a seventy billion model is eleven hundred

375
00:15:46,894 --> 00:15:48,102
twenty gigabytes,

376
00:15:48,102 --> 00:15:50,231
twenty-two dollars forty to move,

377
00:15:50,231 --> 00:15:52,877
five hundred thirty-eight a day if hourly.

378
00:15:53,227 --> 00:15:57,798
Keeping forty-eight hourly checkpoints costs about twelve hundred thirty-six

379
00:15:57,798 --> 00:15:58,941
dollars a month.

380
00:15:58,941 --> 00:16:01,626
Keeping the last three costs seventy-seven,

381
00:16:01,626 --> 00:16:03,283
ninety-four percent less.

382
00:16:03,283 --> 00:16:04,826
Keep the last few full,

383
00:16:04,826 --> 00:16:06,483
every day's weights only,

384
00:16:06,483 --> 00:16:08,482
and expire or archive the rest.

385
00:16:08,482 --> 00:16:09,397
Keep G P Us,

386
00:16:09,397 --> 00:16:11,511
data and checkpoints in one zone.

387
00:16:12,761 --> 00:16:14,265
For ShopNest Copilot.

388
00:16:14,265 --> 00:16:18,358
Put the stable system prompt first and the changing fields last,

389
00:16:18,358 --> 00:16:20,404
and alert on cache read share.

390
00:16:20,404 --> 00:16:23,594
Run offline evals and nightly labelling in batch.

391
00:16:23,594 --> 00:16:26,362
Start on a mid size model with low effort,

392
00:16:26,362 --> 00:16:28,829
and escalate only on a failing check.

393
00:16:29,179 --> 00:16:31,995
Stamp every call at the gateway with team,

394
00:16:31,995 --> 00:16:33,831
feature, tenant and trace.

395
00:16:33,831 --> 00:16:36,034
Report cost per resolved ticket.

396
00:16:36,034 --> 00:16:37,381
Cap spend per key.

397
00:16:37,381 --> 00:16:40,135
Keep the G P Us busy or do not rent them.

398
00:16:40,135 --> 00:16:43,746
And price a hosted open model before building your own.

399
00:16:44,996 --> 00:16:47,014
Four words to carry forward.

400
00:16:47,014 --> 00:16:50,735
Utilisation: the share of paid hours doing useful work.

401
00:16:50,735 --> 00:16:51,681
Real price:

402
00:16:51,681 --> 00:16:54,077
list price divided by utilisation.

403
00:16:54,427 --> 00:16:55,229
Cache hit:

404
00:16:55,229 --> 00:16:58,266
a request whose prompt prefix was already stored,

405
00:16:58,266 --> 00:16:59,698
billed at a fraction.

406
00:16:59,698 --> 00:17:00,729
And unit cost:

407
00:17:00,729 --> 00:17:02,734
the cost of one useful outcome,

408
00:17:02,734 --> 00:17:04,281
like a resolved ticket.

409
00:17:05,531 --> 00:17:06,768
Interview corner.

410
00:17:06,768 --> 00:17:09,596
Asked how you cut the cost of an A I system,

411
00:17:09,596 --> 00:17:11,540
say: fix idle capacity first,

412
00:17:11,540 --> 00:17:14,721
because real price is list price over utilisation.

413
00:17:14,721 --> 00:17:15,723
Then caching,

414
00:17:15,723 --> 00:17:17,254
batch, a prompt audit,

415
00:17:17,254 --> 00:17:18,963
effort, and routing last.

416
00:17:18,963 --> 00:17:22,733
A prompt with a changing field on top got zero percent cache

417
00:17:22,733 --> 00:17:23,911
hits in my test.

418
00:17:24,261 --> 00:17:27,136
Asked whether to self host or use an A P I,

419
00:17:27,136 --> 00:17:29,522
say: compute the break even volume.

420
00:17:29,522 --> 00:17:33,497
One H one hundred beats a premium model above about eleven to

421
00:17:33,497 --> 00:17:35,699
twenty-one million tokens a day,

422
00:17:35,699 --> 00:17:38,146
and never beats a Haiku class model,

423
00:17:38,146 --> 00:17:39,798
so judge cost per task.

424
00:17:41,048 --> 00:17:41,862
Your turn.

425
00:17:41,862 --> 00:17:44,480
The zip for this lesson has four scripts.

426
00:17:44,480 --> 00:17:46,808
The G P U price and commitment sums,

427
00:17:46,808 --> 00:17:48,844
the token cost and cache tests,

428
00:17:48,844 --> 00:17:50,589
the break even calculator,

429
00:17:50,589 --> 00:17:52,393
and the gateway alert test.

430
00:17:52,393 --> 00:17:55,011
Change the prices to your own provider's,

431
00:17:55,011 --> 00:17:57,688
and the utilisation to your own cluster's.

432
00:17:58,038 --> 00:18:01,386
Then move the timestamp in the cache test and watch the hit

433
00:18:01,386 --> 00:18:04,893
rate. One changed number teaches more than an hour of reading.

434
00:18:06,143 --> 00:18:09,601
Recap. Real price is list price over utilisation,

435
00:18:09,601 --> 00:18:13,450
so a three dollar G P U at five percent is sixty-three.

436
00:18:13,450 --> 00:18:17,560
A commitment at discount d needs more than one minus d use.

437
00:18:17,560 --> 00:18:21,670
One training run varied five point four times by how it was

438
00:18:21,670 --> 00:18:22,388
bought.

439
00:18:22,738 --> 00:18:23,630
For tokens,

440
00:18:23,630 --> 00:18:24,582
cache first,

441
00:18:24,582 --> 00:18:25,475
then batch,

442
00:18:25,475 --> 00:18:26,546
audit, effort,

443
00:18:26,546 --> 00:18:27,796
and routing last.

444
00:18:27,796 --> 00:18:30,474
A timestamp on top meant zero cache hits.

445
00:18:30,474 --> 00:18:33,688
One H one hundred never beats a Haiku class A P I.

446
00:18:33,688 --> 00:18:34,938
Stamp every call,

447
00:18:34,938 --> 00:18:37,080
report cost per resolved ticket.

448
00:18:37,080 --> 00:18:38,687
Next, the final lesson,

449
00:18:38,687 --> 00:18:40,056
your learning path.

450
00:18:41,306 --> 00:18:42,493
In the next lesson:

451
00:18:42,493 --> 00:18:43,680
your learning path.

452
00:18:43,680 --> 00:18:45,539
From zero to cloud A I engineer.

453
00:18:45,889 --> 00:18:47,481
Subscribe so you don't miss it.

454
00:18:47,481 --> 00:18:48,755
See you in the notebook.

